<article class="env-lemma" id="04F9">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04F9">Lemma <span data-tag="04F9">37.7.7</span>.</a> Let </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ Z \ar[r]_ h \ar[d]_ f &  X \ar[d]^ g \\  W \ar[r]^{h'} &  Y }  \]
</div>
  <p> be a fibre product diagram in the category of schemes with $h'$ formally unramified and $g$ flat. In this case the corresponding map $Z' \to W'$ of universal first order thickenings is flat, and $f^*\mathcal{C}_{W/Y} \to \mathcal{C}_{Z/X}$ is an isomorphism. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Flatness is preserved under base change, see Morphisms, Lemma <a href="/tag/01U9" data-tag="01U9">29.26.8</a>. Hence the first statement follows from the description of $W'$ in Lemma <a href="/tag/04F8" data-tag="04F8">37.7.6</a>. It is clear that $X \times _ Y W'$ is a first order thickening. It is straightforward to check that it has the universal property because $W'$ has the universal property (by mapping properties of fibre products). See Morphisms, Lemma <a href="/tag/0473" data-tag="0473">29.32.4</a> for why this implies that the map of conormal sheaves is an isomorphism. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>